CS 320 Project Two: Summary and Reflections Report

1. Summary

Unit Testing Approach for Each Feature
For Project One, I developed JUnit tests for the contact, task, and appointment services. My main
approach was to test each service against its requirements and verify that it handled both valid
and invalid inputs correctly. I used positive testing, negative testing, boundary-value analysis,
and equivalence partitioning to check object creation, input validation, and service operations.
These techniques helped me identify potential problems before the services could be used in a
larger application. Boundary-value analysis focuses on testing values at the limits of an allowed
range, while equivalence partitioning groups inputs that are expected to behave similarly
(International Software Testing Qualifications Board [ISTQB], n.d.).
For the contact service, I tested the requirements for contact IDs, names, phone numbers, and
addresses. I checked that required fields were not null and that values did not exceed their
permitted lengths. For example, I tested whether a contact ID with the maximum allowed length
was accepted and whether an ID that exceeded the limit was rejected. I also tested invalid inputs
to confirm that the service prevented incorrect contact information from being stored.
For the task service, I focused on the requirements for task IDs, names, and descriptions. I tested
whether valid task information could be created and whether invalid values were rejected. I also
checked the restrictions on field lengths and required information. For the appointment service, I
tested appointment creation, deletion, and validation. I checked that appointment information
met the requirements, including the restriction that appointment IDs could not exceed 10
characters and that appointment dates could not be in the past. My testing approach aligned with
the software requirements because I used the requirements to determine what each test needed to

