Quality and Effectiveness of JUnit Tests
My contact, task, and appointment services each achieved 100% code coverage. This result
indicates that all executable code measured by my coverage tool was exercised during testing. It
gave me confidence that my tests reached the service methods and validation logic included in
the coverage measurement. However, 100% code coverage does not automatically guarantee that
the software is free of defects. A test can execute a line of code without fully verifying that the
result is correct. For this reason, I also used assertions to check expected outcomes and exception
testing to confirm that invalid inputs were rejected. JUnit provides assertion methods that help
developers compare actual behavior with expected results (JUnit Team, n.d.). Combining
complete code coverage with requirement-based assertions and negative tests gave me a stronger
way to evaluate the quality of my work. I would also review branch coverage separately if it
were available, since 100% code coverage alone does not establish that every possible decision
outcome was tested.
Experience Writing the JUnit Tests
I made sure my tests were technically sound by checking that the expected behavior matched the
requirements. For example, the following test checks that an invalid contact ID causes an
exception:
@Test

void testContactIdTooLongThrowsException() {
Assertions.assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -&gt; {
new Contact(&quot;12345678901&quot;, &quot;John&quot;, &quot;Doe&quot;,
&quot;1234567890&quot;, &quot;123 Main St&quot;);
});
}
This test uses assertThrows() to verify that the constructor rejects an ID that exceeds the 10-
character limit. It is important that the test checks for the expected exception rather than simply
assuming the invalid input will fail. This helps confirm that the application responds to invalid
information as required. I also worked to make my tests efficient and easy to maintain by
separating test cases and using setup methods where appropriate. For example, a @BeforeEach
method can create a fresh service instance before each test. This reduces repeated setup code and
helps prevent one test from affecting another. Keeping tests focused on individual behaviors also
makes it easier to identify the cause of a failure. Together, these practices made my test suite
easier to understand and maintain.

2. Reflection

Testing Techniques
The main testing techniques I used were unit testing, positive testing, negative testing, boundary-
value analysis, and equivalence partitioning. Unit testing allowed me to test individual service
methods and model objects separately. Positive testing checked that valid inputs produced the

