expected results, while negative testing checked that invalid inputs were rejected. These
techniques were useful for verifying the contact, task, and appointment requirements. Boundary-
value analysis focuses on values at the edges of permitted ranges. For example, if a field allows a
maximum of 10 characters, testing values of 10 and 11 characters helps determine whether the
limit is enforced correctly. Equivalence partitioning groups inputs expected to behave similarly.
For example, valid strings and strings that exceed a field&#39;s maximum length can be treated as
separate groups. Instead of testing every possible value, I can select representative values from
each group. These techniques are useful for applications that process forms, user accounts,
financial information, or other data with specific validation rules (ISTQB, n.d.).
There are other testing techniques I did not use extensively in this project. Integration testing
checks whether separate components work correctly together. For example, it could verify that
an appointment service communicates correctly with a database or another scheduling
component. This is useful when individual components pass their unit tests but may still have
problems when connected.
System testing evaluates the complete application against its requirements. For a mobile
application, this could involve creating a contact, scheduling an appointment, and confirming
that the information is displayed correctly throughout the application. Acceptance testing goes a
step further by checking whether the software meets the customer&#39;s needs. A customer or
representative could use acceptance tests to determine whether the finished application supports
the required tasks.
Performance testing measures how well software behaves under different workloads, including
response time and stability. For example, a scheduling system may need to handle thousands of

