Testing Mindset: Caution, Bias, and Discipline
I learned that caution is important because code that works under normal conditions may still fail
when it receives unexpected input. For example, a contact service might create an object
successfully but fail to reject a missing required field. An appointment service might also accept
an invalid date if the validation logic is incomplete. Testing these situations helped me focus on
what could go wrong rather than assuming that the code was correct because it worked in a basic
test.bIt was also important to understand how the services could interact with other parts of an
application. An invalid value accepted by one service could cause problems when another
component uses that information. Even though my project focused on unit testing, I needed to
consider how errors in individual methods could affect the overall application. This reinforced
the importance of validating data and testing each requirement carefully.
I also tried to limit bias by checking the code against the requirements instead of relying on my
assumptions. Developers may naturally focus on successful outcomes because they expect their
code to work. To reduce this bias, I tested invalid inputs, checked field restrictions, and used
assertions to compare actual behavior with expected results. For example, testing both valid and
invalid contact IDs helped me evaluate whether the validation worked correctly in both
situations. In a professional environment, I would also welcome code reviews and independent

