testing because another developer may notice problems that I overlooked. Discipline is important
because cutting corners can create technical debt. Technical debt occurs when shortcuts or
unresolved defects make future development more difficult. For example, failing to validate an
appointment date could allow incorrect information into the system and require additional fixes
later. Skipping tests might save time initially, but it could lead to more expensive problems when
the application is updated or used by customers.
To avoid unnecessary technical debt, I plan to review requirements before coding, write tests for
valid and invalid inputs, and run my test suite after making changes. I will also maintain clear
test names, use version control, review code coverage, and fix defects instead of leaving them
unresolved. My 100% code coverage results are an important accomplishment, but I understand
that maintaining quality requires more than reaching a percentage. As a software engineering
professional, I need to remain careful, objective, and consistent so that my code is reliable,
maintainable, and aligned with customer requirements.

